    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Biology</span> is the scientific study of life. To study life, we must first understand what it means to be <span class=\"learn-keyword\">alive</span>. Living things — also called <span class=\"learn-keyword\">organisms</span> — share a set of characteristics that distinguish them from non-living things. Recognizing these characteristics is the foundation of all biological study.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Characteristics of Living Things (MRGREN)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The acronym <strong>MRGREN</strong> summarizes the key life processes:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Process</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>M</td><td>Movement</td><td>Ability to move whole body or parts</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>R</td><td>Respiration</td><td>Release of energy from food</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>G</td><td>Growth</td><td>Permanent increase in size/mass</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>R</td><td>Reproduction</td><td>Producing offspring of same kind</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>E</td><td>Excretion</td><td>Removal of metabolic waste products</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>N</td><td>Nutrition</td><td>Obtaining and using food/energy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>+ S</td><td>Sensitivity</td><td>Detecting and responding to stimuli</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Non-Living Things</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Non-living things do not carry out life processes. They may show one or two features that resemble life (e.g., fire grows and uses oxygen, a car moves) but they do <strong>not</strong> possess all the characteristics simultaneously. The key distinction is that living organisms carry out <strong>all</strong> life processes and are made of cells.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Differences Between Living and Non-Living Things</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Living Things</th><th>Non-Living Things</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Cellular structure</td><td>Made of one or more cells</td><td>Not made of cells</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Metabolism</td><td>Chemical reactions occur inside</td><td>No metabolic reactions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Growth</td><td>Grow from within (intrinsic)</td><td>May increase by accumulation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Reproduction</td><td>Can produce own kind</td><td>Cannot reproduce</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Response</td><td>Respond to stimuli</td><td>Do not respond to stimuli</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Need and process food</td><td>Do not need food</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Special Cases: Viruses</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Viruses are a special category — they show some features of living things (reproduction inside host cells, genetic material) but lack others (no cellular structure, no independent metabolism). They are therefore considered on the <strong>borderline between living and non-living</strong>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-svg-wrap\">\n  <svg class=\"learn-svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <rect width=\"480\" height=\"200\" fill=\"#0f0020\" rx=\"10\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Characteristics of Living Things</text>\n    <circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"110\" r=\"75\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#28c840\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#28c840\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">LIVING ORGANISM</text>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"9\">Movement · Respiration</text>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"9\">Growth · Reproduction</text>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"9\">Excretion · Nutrition</text>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8b4f0\" font-size=\"9\">Sensitivity · Made of cells</text>\n    <rect x=\"10\" y=\"150\" width=\"130\" height=\"35\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#1a0030\" stroke=\"#D4AF37\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"75\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" font-size=\"9\">Non-Living: fire</text>\n    <text x=\"75\" y=\"178\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">moves but not alive</text>\n    <rect x=\"340\" y=\"150\" width=\"130\" height=\"35\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#1a0030\" stroke=\"#c8b4f0\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"405\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8b4f0\" font-size=\"9\">Virus: borderline</text>\n    <text x=\"405\" y=\"178\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">not truly living/non-living</text>\n  </svg>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Examiners often ask about viruses or fire as tricky cases. Remember: viruses are not cells, cannot grow or metabolize independently, so they are NOT classified as living. Fire uses oxygen and grows but does not reproduce its own kind or have cells.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Living things are distinguished by seven characteristics: movement, respiration, growth, reproduction, excretion, nutrition, and sensitivity (MR GRENS). They are all made of cells and carry out metabolism. Non-living things may show one or two of these features but never all. Viruses are borderline cases.\n</div>\n  ",
