    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Classification</span> (taxonomy) is the systematic grouping of organisms based on their similarities and differences. It helps biologists organize the enormous diversity of life — estimated at over 8 million species — into a logical, manageable system. The science of classification is called <span class=\"learn-keyword\">taxonomy</span>, and its pioneer was <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Carl Linnaeus</span> (1707–1778).\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Importance of Classification</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Makes the study of the enormous diversity of life manageable and systematic</li>\n  <li>Enables scientists worldwide to communicate using the same names for organisms</li>\n  <li>Reveals evolutionary relationships between organisms</li>\n  <li>Helps in identifying and naming new species</li>\n  <li>Assists in agriculture, medicine, and conservation by grouping organisms with similar properties</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Levels (Hierarchy) of Classification</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Organisms are arranged in a hierarchy from the most general to the most specific. The seven main levels are:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Level (Taxon)</th><th>Description</th><th>Example (Human)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Kingdom</td><td>Broadest grouping</td><td>Animalia</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Phylum</td><td>Major body plan grouping</td><td>Chordata</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Class</td><td>More specific grouping</td><td>Mammalia</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Order</td><td>Related families</td><td>Primates</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Family</td><td>Closely related genera</td><td>Hominidae</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Genus</td><td>Very closely related species</td><td>Homo</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Species</td><td>Most specific; organisms can interbreed</td><td>sapiens</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Mnemonic:</strong> <em>King Philip Came Over For Good Soup</em> (Kingdom → Phylum → Class → Order → Family → Genus → Species)</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Binomial Nomenclature</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Linnaeus introduced the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">binomial nomenclature</span> system — giving each organism a two-part scientific name consisting of <strong>Genus</strong> and <strong>species</strong>. Rules:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Written in Latin or Latinized form</li>\n  <li>Genus name starts with a capital letter; species name is lowercase</li>\n  <li>Both are italicized when printed (or underlined when handwritten)</li>\n  <li>Example: <em>Homo sapiens</em> (human), <em>Zea mays</em> (maize), <em>Canis lupus familiaris</em> (dog)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Basis of Classification</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Organisms are classified based on:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Morphology:</strong> Physical appearance and body structure</li>\n  <li><strong>Anatomy:</strong> Internal structure</li>\n  <li><strong>Biochemistry:</strong> Chemical composition and reactions</li>\n  <li><strong>Genetics:</strong> DNA sequences and chromosomes</li>\n  <li><strong>Evolutionary history:</strong> Common ancestry (phylogeny)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-svg-wrap\">\n  <svg class=\"learn-svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <rect width=\"480\" height=\"200\" fill=\"#0f0020\" rx=\"10\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Hierarchy of Classification</text>\n    <rect x=\"185\" y=\"28\" width=\"110\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#4B0082\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"41\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\">KINGDOM (broadest)</text>\n    <rect x=\"170\" y=\"50\" width=\"140\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#28c840\" fill-opacity=\"0.7\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\">PHYLUM</text>\n    <rect x=\"160\" y=\"70\" width=\"160\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" fill-opacity=\"0.7\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#0f0020\" font-size=\"9\">CLASS</text>\n    <rect x=\"150\" y=\"90\" width=\"180\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#c8b4f0\" fill-opacity=\"0.7\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#0f0020\" font-size=\"9\">ORDER</text>\n    <rect x=\"140\" y=\"110\" width=\"200\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#28c840\" fill-opacity=\"0.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\">FAMILY</text>\n    <rect x=\"130\" y=\"130\" width=\"220\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" fill-opacity=\"0.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#0f0020\" font-size=\"9\">GENUS</text>\n    <rect x=\"120\" y=\"150\" width=\"240\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#4B0082\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\">SPECIES (most specific)</text>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"188\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">King Philip Came Over For Good Soup</text>\n  </svg>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember that a species is defined as a group of organisms that can interbreed and produce fertile offspring. The scientific name of an organism always has the genus first (capital letter) and species second (lowercase), both in italics.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Classification organizes life into a hierarchy of seven levels: Kingdom, Phylum, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species. Linnaeus developed binomial nomenclature — each organism has a two-part Latin name (genus + species). Classification is based on morphology, anatomy, biochemistry, genetics, and evolutionary history.\n</div>\n  ",
