    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Modern biology organizes all life into <span class=\"learn-keyword\">five kingdoms</span>: Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, and Animalia. Each kingdom is defined by fundamental differences in cell structure, nutrition, body organization, and mode of reproduction. Understanding the five kingdoms is essential to understanding the diversity of life on Earth.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Kingdom Monera (Prokaryotes)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Monerans are the simplest and most ancient organisms. They are <strong>prokaryotes</strong> — their cells lack a true membrane-bound nucleus.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Examples:</strong> Bacteria (e.g., <em>Escherichia coli</em>) and Cyanobacteria (blue-green algae)</li>\n  <li><strong>Cell type:</strong> Prokaryotic (no nuclear membrane, no membrane-bound organelles)</li>\n  <li><strong>Body organization:</strong> Unicellular</li>\n  <li><strong>Nutrition:</strong> Autotrophic (photosynthetic or chemosynthetic) or heterotrophic (saprophytic or parasitic)</li>\n  <li><strong>Reproduction:</strong> Binary fission (asexual)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Kingdom Protista</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Protists are mostly <strong>unicellular eukaryotes</strong> — they have a true nucleus but are generally single-celled.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Examples:</strong> Amoeba, Paramecium, Euglena, Plasmodium (malaria parasite), algae</li>\n  <li><strong>Cell type:</strong> Eukaryotic (true nucleus present)</li>\n  <li><strong>Body organization:</strong> Mostly unicellular; some colonial or multicellular</li>\n  <li><strong>Nutrition:</strong> Autotrophic (e.g., algae) or heterotrophic (e.g., Amoeba)</li>\n  <li><strong>Euglena</strong> is a special case — it is both autotrophic (has chlorophyll) and heterotrophic (can ingest food in darkness)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Kingdom Fungi</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Fungi are mostly multicellular, eukaryotic organisms that cannot photosynthesize — they obtain nutrients by absorption.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Examples:</strong> Mushrooms, moulds, yeasts, <em>Rhizopus</em> (bread mould), <em>Aspergillus</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Cell type:</strong> Eukaryotic; cell walls made of chitin (not cellulose)</li>\n  <li><strong>Body structure:</strong> Made of thread-like hyphae forming a mycelium</li>\n  <li><strong>Nutrition:</strong> Saprophytic (absorb from dead matter) or parasitic</li>\n  <li><strong>Reproduction:</strong> Spores (asexual and sexual)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Kingdom Plantae</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Plants are multicellular eukaryotes with cell walls made of cellulose. They are autotrophic — they make food by photosynthesis.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Examples:</strong> Mosses, ferns, conifers, flowering plants</li>\n  <li><strong>Cell type:</strong> Eukaryotic; cell wall of cellulose; chloroplasts present</li>\n  <li><strong>Nutrition:</strong> Autotrophic (photosynthesis)</li>\n  <li><strong>Body organization:</strong> Multicellular; organized into tissues and organs</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Kingdom Animalia</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Animals are multicellular eukaryotes without cell walls. They are heterotrophic — they consume other organisms for food.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Examples:</strong> Insects, fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds, mammals</li>\n  <li><strong>Cell type:</strong> Eukaryotic; no cell wall; no chloroplasts</li>\n  <li><strong>Nutrition:</strong> Heterotrophic (ingestive feeding)</li>\n  <li><strong>Movement:</strong> Most can move from place to place (locomotion)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Kingdom</th><th>Cell Type</th><th>Cell Wall</th><th>Nutrition</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Monera</td><td>Prokaryotic</td><td>Peptidoglycan</td><td>Auto/Hetero</td><td>Bacteria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Protista</td><td>Eukaryotic</td><td>Some have</td><td>Auto/Hetero</td><td>Amoeba, Euglena</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fungi</td><td>Eukaryotic</td><td>Chitin</td><td>Saprophytic</td><td>Mushroom</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Plantae</td><td>Eukaryotic</td><td>Cellulose</td><td>Autotrophic</td><td>Mango tree</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Animalia</td><td>Eukaryotic</td><td>None</td><td>Heterotrophic</td><td>Human</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The key difference between Monera and the other four kingdoms is that Monera are PROKARYOTES (no true nucleus). All others are eukaryotes. Remember that Fungi cell walls contain CHITIN (not cellulose like plants).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The five kingdoms are Monera (prokaryotes), Protista (mostly unicellular eukaryotes), Fungi (absorptive heterotrophs, chitin cell walls), Plantae (photosynthetic, cellulose cell walls), and Animalia (ingestive heterotrophs, no cell walls). Euglena is a protist showing both plant-like and animal-like features.\n</div>\n  ",
