    "module": "Cell Biology",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">cell</span> is the basic structural and functional unit of all living organisms. This is the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">cell theory</span>, formulated by Schleiden (1838), Schwann (1839), and Virchow (1855). Every living organism is made of one or more cells, and all cells arise from pre-existing cells.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Cells</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">There are two fundamental cell types:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Prokaryotic Cell</th><th>Eukaryotic Cell</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Nucleus</td><td>No membrane-bound nucleus; nucleoid region</td><td>True nucleus with nuclear membrane</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Size</td><td>Smaller (1–10 µm)</td><td>Larger (10–100 µm)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Organelles</td><td>No membrane-bound organelles</td><td>Membrane-bound organelles present</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>DNA</td><td>Circular, in cytoplasm</td><td>Linear, enclosed in nucleus</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Examples</td><td>Bacteria, cyanobacteria</td><td>Plant, animal, fungal cells</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Cell Organelles and Their Functions</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Organelle</th><th>Function</th><th>Present In</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Cell membrane</td><td>Controls entry/exit of substances (selective permeability)</td><td>All cells</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cell wall</td><td>Provides structural support and shape</td><td>Plants, fungi, bacteria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nucleus</td><td>Controls cell activities; contains DNA (genetic information)</td><td>Eukaryotes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nucleolus</td><td>Produces ribosomal RNA (rRNA)</td><td>Eukaryotes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cytoplasm</td><td>Medium where chemical reactions occur; suspends organelles</td><td>All cells</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mitochondria</td><td>Site of aerobic respiration; produces ATP (\"powerhouse of cell\")</td><td>Eukaryotes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chloroplast</td><td>Site of photosynthesis; contains chlorophyll</td><td>Plant cells only</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ribosomes</td><td>Site of protein synthesis</td><td>All cells</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Endoplasmic reticulum (ER)</td><td>Transport network; rough ER has ribosomes (protein); smooth ER (lipids)</td><td>Eukaryotes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Golgi apparatus</td><td>Packages and secretes proteins and lipids</td><td>Eukaryotes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Lysosomes</td><td>Digestion of waste materials and foreign substances</td><td>Animal cells mainly</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Vacuole</td><td>Storage (water, food, waste); large central vacuole in plants</td><td>All cells (large in plants)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Centrosome/Centrioles</td><td>Cell division (forms spindle fibres)</td><td>Animal cells mainly</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Plant vs Animal Cells</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Plant Cell</th><th>Animal Cell</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Cell wall</td><td>Present (cellulose)</td><td>Absent</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chloroplasts</td><td>Present</td><td>Absent</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Vacuole</td><td>Large, permanent, central</td><td>Small or absent</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Centrioles</td><td>Absent (in most)</td><td>Present</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Shape</td><td>Regular, fixed</td><td>Irregular, flexible</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-svg-wrap\">\n  <svg class=\"learn-svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <rect width=\"480\" height=\"200\" fill=\"#0f0020\" rx=\"10\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">Plant Cell vs Animal Cell</text>\n    <!-- Plant Cell -->\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"25\" width=\"215\" height=\"160\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1a0030\" stroke=\"#28c840\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"42\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#28c840\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">PLANT CELL</text>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"58\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">✔ Cell wall (cellulose)</text>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">✔ Chloroplasts</text>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">✔ Large central vacuole</text>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">✔ Nucleus, mitochondria</text>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">✔ Fixed rectangular shape</text>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" font-size=\"8\">✘ No centrioles (usually)</text>\n    <!-- Animal Cell -->\n    <rect x=\"250\" y=\"25\" width=\"215\" height=\"160\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1a0030\" stroke=\"#c8b4f0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"42\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8b4f0\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">ANIMAL CELL</text>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"58\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">✔ Centrioles</text>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">✔ Lysosomes</text>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">✔ Nucleus, mitochondria</text>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">✔ Irregular shape (flexible)</text>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" font-size=\"8\">✘ No cell wall</text>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" font-size=\"8\">✘ No chloroplasts</text>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" font-size=\"8\">✘ No large vacuole</text>\n  </svg>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The three organelles found in plant cells but NOT in animal cells are: cell wall, chloroplasts, and large permanent central vacuole. Centrioles are found in animal cells but not (usually) in plant cells.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Cells are the basic units of life (cell theory). Prokaryotic cells (bacteria) lack a true nucleus; eukaryotic cells have a true membrane-bound nucleus. Organelles have specific functions: mitochondria = energy (ATP), chloroplasts = photosynthesis, ribosomes = protein synthesis, nucleus = control center (DNA). Plant cells have cell walls (cellulose), chloroplasts, and large vacuoles; animal cells have centrioles and lysosomes.\n</div>\n  ",
