    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Cells constantly interact with their surroundings. Three key processes govern how substances move into and out of cells: <span class=\"learn-keyword\">diffusion</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">osmosis</span>, and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">active transport</span>. Understanding these processes explains how cells obtain nutrients, expel waste, and maintain their internal balance (homeostasis).\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Diffusion</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Diffusion is the movement of molecules or ions from a region of <strong>higher concentration</strong> to a region of <strong>lower concentration</strong> down the concentration gradient. It requires <strong>no energy</strong> (passive process).</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Occurs in gases, liquids, and across cell membranes</li>\n  <li>Rate depends on: concentration gradient, temperature, surface area, and molecular size</li>\n  <li><strong>Examples:</strong> O₂ entering cells; CO₂ leaving cells; perfume spreading in a room</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Osmosis</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Osmosis is the diffusion of <strong>water molecules</strong> through a <span class=\"learn-keyword\">selectively permeable membrane</span> from a region of <strong>lower solute concentration</strong> (higher water potential) to a region of <strong>higher solute concentration</strong> (lower water potential).</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Only water molecules move (not solutes)</li>\n  <li>Requires no energy (passive process)</li>\n  <li><strong>Hypotonic solution:</strong> Less concentrated than cell → water enters cell → cell swells (may burst in animal cells = lysis; plant cells become turgid)</li>\n  <li><strong>Hypertonic solution:</strong> More concentrated than cell → water leaves cell → cell shrinks (plasmolysis in plants; crenation in animal cells)</li>\n  <li><strong>Isotonic solution:</strong> Same concentration as cell → no net movement of water</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Active Transport</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Active transport is the movement of molecules or ions from a region of <strong>lower concentration</strong> to a region of <strong>higher concentration</strong> — <em>against</em> the concentration gradient. It <strong>requires energy (ATP)</strong> and involves <strong>carrier proteins</strong>.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Examples: absorption of glucose and amino acids from the gut; uptake of mineral ions by root hair cells; sodium-potassium pump in nerve cells</li>\n  <li>Requires metabolic energy (ATP) and specific carrier proteins in the membrane</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Diffusion</th><th>Osmosis</th><th>Active Transport</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Substance</td><td>Any molecule/ion</td><td>Water only</td><td>Any molecule/ion</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Direction</td><td>High → Low conc.</td><td>High water → Low water potential</td><td>Low → High conc. (against gradient)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Energy needed</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (ATP)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Membrane needed</td><td>Not always</td><td>Yes (selectively permeable)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Carrier proteins</td><td>No (simple diffusion)</td><td>No (aquaporins)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Osmosis is a special type of diffusion involving ONLY water through a selectively permeable membrane. Remember: water moves from LESS concentrated solution (more water) to MORE concentrated solution (less water). Plants become turgid in hypotonic solutions (beneficial — maintains shape) and plasmolyse in hypertonic solutions.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Three ways substances cross cell membranes: (1) Diffusion — passive, any molecule, high to low concentration. (2) Osmosis — passive, water only, through selectively permeable membrane, from high to low water potential. (3) Active transport — requires ATP, against concentration gradient, uses carrier proteins. Examples include root mineral uptake (active) and gas exchange in lungs (diffusion).\n</div>\n  ",
