    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Every living cell carries out a set of fundamental life processes: <span class=\"learn-keyword\">nutrition</span> (obtaining energy-containing substances), <span class=\"learn-keyword\">respiration</span> (releasing energy from food), <span class=\"learn-keyword\">growth</span> (increasing in size), and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">irritability</span> (responding to stimuli). These properties define the cell as a living unit.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Nutrition</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Nutrition is the process by which cells obtain energy-containing substances (food) needed for metabolic activities.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Autotrophic nutrition:</strong> Cells make their own food using light (photosynthesis) or chemical energy (chemosynthesis) — e.g., plant cells with chloroplasts</li>\n  <li><strong>Heterotrophic nutrition:</strong> Cells obtain energy by consuming other organisms — includes holozoic (ingestion), saprophytic (absorption), and parasitic nutrition</li>\n  <li>Nutrients obtained include: carbohydrates, proteins, lipids, minerals, vitamins, and water</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Respiration at the Cellular Level</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Cellular respiration releases energy from glucose. It occurs in the cytoplasm (glycolysis) and mitochondria (Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation).</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Aerobic:</strong> C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + ATP (large amount of ATP)</li>\n  <li><strong>Anaerobic (in animals/yeast):</strong> Glucose → Lactic acid + small ATP (animals) or Ethanol + CO₂ + small ATP (yeast)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Growth</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Cells grow by:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Cell division (mitosis):</strong> One cell divides into two identical daughter cells — increases cell number</li>\n  <li><strong>Cell enlargement:</strong> Cells take in more water and synthesize more cytoplasm — increases cell size</li>\n  <li><strong>Cell differentiation:</strong> Cells become specialized for specific functions</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Irritability (Sensitivity)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Irritability is the ability of a cell to detect and respond to changes (stimuli) in its environment.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Nerve cells detect electrical and chemical signals</li>\n  <li>Muscle cells respond to nerve impulses by contracting</li>\n  <li>Plant cells respond to light (phototropism), gravity (geotropism), and water (hydrotropism)</li>\n  <li>Unicellular organisms like Amoeba respond to chemicals (chemotaxis) and light (phototaxis)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Definition</th><th>Cellular Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Obtaining food/energy</td><td>Chloroplast photosynthesis; glucose absorption</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Respiration</td><td>Releasing energy from food</td><td>Mitochondria producing ATP</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Growth</td><td>Increase in size/number</td><td>Cell division (mitosis); cell enlargement</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Irritability</td><td>Response to stimuli</td><td>Nerve impulse; phototropism</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Distinguish between nutrition (obtaining food) and respiration (releasing energy from food). Nutrition provides the raw materials; respiration extracts the energy. Both are essential cellular properties.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The four key properties of a living cell are nutrition, respiration, growth, and irritability. Autotrophic cells make their own food; heterotrophic cells consume others. Respiration releases ATP from glucose in mitochondria. Growth involves cell division (mitosis) and enlargement. Irritability enables cells to respond to environmental stimuli.\n</div>\n  ",
