    "topic": "Respiration",
    "topicCode": "SS1-BIO-07",
    "module": "Cell Biology",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Respiration</span> is the chemical process by which living cells break down organic molecules (mainly glucose) to release energy in the form of <span class=\"learn-keyword\">ATP</span>. It occurs in every living cell and must not be confused with breathing (ventilation), which is the mechanical process of moving air in and out of the lungs.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Aerobic Respiration</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Aerobic respiration requires oxygen and releases a large amount of energy (ATP). It occurs in two main stages:</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Stage 1 — Glycolysis</strong> (in cytoplasm): Glucose (6C) → 2 pyruvate (3C) + small ATP + NADH</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Stage 2 — Krebs Cycle + Oxidative Phosphorylation</strong> (in mitochondria): Pyruvate is fully oxidized to CO₂ + H₂O, releasing large amounts of ATP</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Overall equation:</strong></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><strong>C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + ~36–38 ATP</strong></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Sites:</strong> Cytoplasm (glycolysis) and Mitochondria (Krebs cycle and ETC)</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Anaerobic Respiration</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Anaerobic respiration occurs <strong>without oxygen</strong> and releases much less energy. Two types:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Organism</th><th>Products</th><th>Equation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Animals (muscle cells)</td><td>Lactic acid + small ATP</td><td>Glucose → 2 lactic acid + 2 ATP</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Yeast/microorganisms</td><td>Ethanol + CO₂ + small ATP</td><td>Glucose → 2 ethanol + 2CO₂ + 2 ATP</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Comparison: Aerobic vs Anaerobic Respiration</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Aerobic</th><th>Anaerobic</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Oxygen required</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>ATP yield</td><td>36–38 ATP per glucose</td><td>2 ATP per glucose</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Products</td><td>CO₂ + H₂O</td><td>Lactic acid (animals) or ethanol + CO₂ (yeast)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Site</td><td>Cytoplasm + Mitochondria</td><td>Cytoplasm only</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Duration</td><td>Sustained</td><td>Short-term only</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Practical Importance of Respiration</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Fermentation (yeast anaerobic):</strong> Bread-making (CO₂ makes dough rise); brewing beer and wine (ethanol)</li>\n  <li><strong>Muscle fatigue:</strong> Lactic acid accumulation during intense exercise → oxygen debt</li>\n  <li><strong>Germination:</strong> Seeds respire rapidly to provide energy for growth</li>\n  <li><strong>Body temperature:</strong> Respiration releases heat, helping warm-blooded animals maintain body temperature</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-svg-wrap\">\n  <svg class=\"learn-svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 190\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <rect width=\"480\" height=\"190\" fill=\"#0f0020\" rx=\"10\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">Respiration Summary</text>\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"28\" width=\"215\" height=\"145\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1a0030\" stroke=\"#28c840\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"46\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#28c840\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">AEROBIC</text>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">Glucose + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O + ATP</text>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"76\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" font-size=\"8\">Yield: ~36–38 ATP per glucose</text>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">Stage 1: Glycolysis (cytoplasm)</text>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">Stage 2: Krebs (mitochondria)</text>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">Stage 3: ETC (mitochondria)</text>\n    <text x=\"122\" y=\"135\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#28c840\" font-size=\"8\">Complete oxidation of glucose</text>\n    <rect x=\"250\" y=\"28\" width=\"215\" height=\"145\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#1a0030\" stroke=\"#c8b4f0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"46\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8b4f0\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">ANAEROBIC</text>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">No oxygen required</text>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"76\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#D4AF37\" font-size=\"8\">Yield: ~2 ATP per glucose (low)</text>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">Animals: glucose → lactic acid</text>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">Yeast: glucose → ethanol + CO₂</text>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8c8c8\" font-size=\"8\">Only cytoplasm involved</text>\n    <text x=\"357\" y=\"135\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c8b4f0\" font-size=\"8\">Used in brewing, bread-making</text>\n  </svg>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: respiration is NOT the same as breathing. Respiration = chemical release of energy from food in EVERY cell. Breathing = mechanical movement of air into/out of lungs. Aerobic respiration produces FAR more ATP (36–38) than anaerobic (2 ATP).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Aerobic respiration: C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + 36–38 ATP (cytoplasm + mitochondria). Anaerobic respiration: glucose → lactic acid (animals) or ethanol + CO₂ (yeast) + 2 ATP (cytoplasm only). Aerobic yields far more ATP. Fermentation (yeast anaerobic) is used in brewing and bread-making.\n</div>\n  ",
