    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">tissue</span> is a group of similar cells that work together to perform a specific function. The study of tissues is called <span class=\"learn-keyword\">histology</span>. In multicellular organisms, cells are organized into tissues, tissues into organs, organs into organ systems, and systems into the complete organism.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Levels of Organisation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Cell → Tissue → Organ → Organ System → Organism</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Cell:</strong> Basic unit (e.g., nerve cell, muscle cell)</li>\n  <li><strong>Tissue:</strong> Group of similar cells with same function (e.g., muscle tissue)</li>\n  <li><strong>Organ:</strong> Group of tissues working together (e.g., heart, leaf)</li>\n  <li><strong>Organ System:</strong> Group of organs with related functions (e.g., digestive system)</li>\n  <li><strong>Organism:</strong> Complete living individual</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Animal Tissues</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Tissue Type</th><th>Function</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Epithelial</td><td>Covers body surfaces; protection, absorption, secretion</td><td>Skin, gut lining, lung alveoli</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Connective</td><td>Supports, binds, and connects other tissues</td><td>Bone, cartilage, blood, tendons</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Muscle</td><td>Contraction for movement</td><td>Skeletal, smooth, cardiac muscle</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nervous</td><td>Transmits electrical impulses for communication</td><td>Neurons (brain, spinal cord, nerves)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Types of Plant Tissues</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Tissue Type</th><th>Function</th><th>Example/Location</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Meristematic</td><td>Cell division for growth</td><td>Root and shoot tips, cambium</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Epidermal</td><td>Protection; reduces water loss</td><td>Outer layer of leaves and stems</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Parenchyma</td><td>Photosynthesis, storage</td><td>Mesophyll of leaves</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Collenchyma</td><td>Flexible support in young stems</td><td>Stems of herbaceous plants</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sclerenchyma</td><td>Rigid support and strength (dead cells)</td><td>Fibres in stems; seed coats</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Xylem</td><td>Transport water and minerals upward</td><td>Vascular bundles of stems</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Phloem</td><td>Transport sugars (sucrose) to all parts</td><td>Vascular bundles of stems</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Supporting Systems</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Supporting systems provide structural integrity. In animals, the skeleton supports the body. Two types:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Exoskeleton:</strong> External skeleton made of chitin (e.g., insects, crustaceans)</li>\n  <li><strong>Endoskeleton:</strong> Internal skeleton made of bone/cartilage (e.g., vertebrates including humans)</li>\n  <li>Plants are supported by: turgor pressure in cells, cellulose cell walls, collenchyma/sclerenchyma tissues, and woody tissue (xylem)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Know the four types of animal tissues: Epithelial, Connective, Muscle, Nervous (ECMN). Know that Xylem transports WATER (upward) and Phloem transports SUGARS (sucrose — both up and down). Xylem cells are dead; phloem cells are living.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Tissues are groups of similar cells performing a common function. Animals have four main tissue types: epithelial, connective, muscle, and nervous. Plants have meristematic, epidermal, parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma, xylem, and phloem tissues. Supporting systems (skeleton in animals, turgor/cell walls in plants) provide structural integrity.\n</div>\n  ",
