    "topic": "Nutrition",
    "topicCode": "SS1-BIO-10",
    "module": "Nutrition and Health",
    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Nutrition</span> is the process by which organisms obtain and use food for energy, growth, and maintenance of life. There are two fundamental types: <span class=\"learn-keyword\">autotrophic</span> (self-feeding) and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">heterotrophic</span> (other-feeding). Understanding these types reveals how energy flows through ecosystems.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Autotrophic Nutrition</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Autotrophs manufacture their own food from simple inorganic substances.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Photosynthesis (Photoautotrophs):</strong> Use light energy to convert CO₂ + H₂O into glucose. Examples: green plants, algae, cyanobacteria</li>\n  <li><strong>Chemosynthesis (Chemoautotrophs):</strong> Use chemical energy from oxidizing inorganic compounds. Examples: nitrifying bacteria (<em>Nitrosomonas, Nitrobacter</em>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Photosynthesis equation:</strong> 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + light energy → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Heterotrophic Nutrition</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Heterotrophs cannot make their own food and must consume organic matter.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Method</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Holozoic</td><td>Ingesting solid food; full digestion</td><td>Humans, most animals</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Saprophytic (Saprotrophic)</td><td>Secreting enzymes; absorbing products from dead matter</td><td>Fungi, many bacteria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Parasitic</td><td>Living on/in a host; obtaining food at host's expense</td><td>Tapeworm, <em>Plasmodium</em>, mistletoe</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Symbiotic (mutualistic)</td><td>Both organisms benefit</td><td>Nitrogen-fixing bacteria in legume roots</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Holozoic Nutrition — Five Stages</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Ingestion:</strong> Taking food into the mouth/body</li>\n  <li><strong>Digestion:</strong> Breaking down complex molecules (mechanical + chemical)</li>\n  <li><strong>Absorption:</strong> Taking up digested nutrients into the blood/lymph</li>\n  <li><strong>Assimilation:</strong> Using absorbed nutrients in metabolism</li>\n  <li><strong>Egestion:</strong> Expelling undigested food as faeces</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Classes of Food Nutrients</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Nutrient</th><th>Function</th><th>Example Sources</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Carbohydrates</td><td>Primary energy source</td><td>Rice, yam, bread</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Proteins</td><td>Growth, repair, enzymes, hormones</td><td>Meat, fish, beans</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Lipids (Fats/Oils)</td><td>Energy reserve, insulation, cell membranes</td><td>Palm oil, groundnut</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Vitamins</td><td>Regulate metabolic reactions</td><td>Fruits, vegetables</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Minerals</td><td>Structural (Ca, P in bones); regulatory (Na, K)</td><td>Milk, green leafy vegetables</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Water</td><td>Solvent; transport medium; reactions</td><td>All foods, drinking water</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Roughage (fibre)</td><td>Aids bowel movement, prevents constipation</td><td>Vegetables, whole grains</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember that photosynthesis is the REVERSE of aerobic respiration in terms of reactants and products: Photosynthesis consumes CO₂ and produces O₂; respiration consumes O₂ and produces CO₂. Autotrophs are the PRODUCERS in food chains.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Autotrophs (plants, algae) make food via photosynthesis or chemosynthesis. Heterotrophs obtain food by holozoic (ingestion), saprophytic (absorption from dead matter), or parasitic nutrition. Holozoic nutrition has 5 stages: ingestion → digestion → absorption → assimilation → egestion. The 7 classes of nutrients are carbohydrates, proteins, lipids, vitamins, minerals, water, and roughage.\n</div>\n  ",
