    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Ecology</span> is the scientific study of the relationships between living organisms and their environment. It examines how organisms interact with each other and with the physical (abiotic) factors of their habitat. Key concepts include habitat, ecosystem, biotic factors, and abiotic factors.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Habitat</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">habitat</span> is the specific place or locality where an organism normally lives and finds what it needs to survive (food, water, shelter, mates). Examples: a pond, a forest, the soil, a coral reef.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Ecosystem</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">An <span class=\"learn-keyword\">ecosystem</span> is a community of living organisms (biotic component) interacting with the non-living (abiotic) components of their environment as a functional unit. Examples: a pond ecosystem, forest ecosystem, savanna ecosystem.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Biotic component:</strong> All living organisms (producers, consumers, decomposers)</li>\n  <li><strong>Abiotic component:</strong> Non-living physical factors (temperature, light, water, soil, pH, wind)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Ecological Factors</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Abiotic Factors</th><th>Effect on Organisms</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Light intensity</td><td>Affects photosynthesis rate; determines plant distribution</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Temperature</td><td>Affects enzyme activity and metabolic rates</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Water/humidity</td><td>Essential for life; drought limits distribution</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Soil composition (edaphic)</td><td>pH, mineral content, texture affect plant growth</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Wind</td><td>Pollination, seed dispersal, affects water loss</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Salinity</td><td>High salinity limits freshwater organisms</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Biotic Factors</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Competition:</strong> Organisms competing for the same limited resource</li>\n  <li><strong>Predation:</strong> One organism (predator) killing and eating another (prey)</li>\n  <li><strong>Parasitism:</strong> One organism (parasite) benefits; host is harmed</li>\n  <li><strong>Mutualism:</strong> Both organisms benefit</li>\n  <li><strong>Commensalism:</strong> One benefits; the other is neither helped nor harmed</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Population, Community, Biome, Biosphere</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Population</td><td>All individuals of one species in an area at a time</td><td>All grasshoppers in a field</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Community</td><td>All populations of different species in an area</td><td>All organisms in a pond</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ecosystem</td><td>Community + abiotic factors</td><td>Pond + its water, light, temperature</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Biome</td><td>Large geographic area with characteristic climate and organisms</td><td>Tropical rainforest, savanna</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Biosphere</td><td>All parts of Earth supporting life</td><td>Entire Earth's life zone</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: Population (one species) → Community (all species) → Ecosystem (community + abiotic) → Biome → Biosphere. Abiotic means non-living; biotic means living. Ecology studies interactions BETWEEN organisms AND between organisms and their abiotic environment.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Ecology is the study of organisms and their environment. A habitat is where an organism lives. An ecosystem includes biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) components. Abiotic factors include light, temperature, water, soil. Biotic factors include competition, predation, parasitism, mutualism. The hierarchy is: Population → Community → Ecosystem → Biome → Biosphere.\n</div>\n  ",
