    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">population</span> is all the individuals of the same species living in a defined area at a given time. Population ecology examines how populations change in size over time, what factors control these changes, and how populations interact with their environment.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Population Density</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Population density</strong> is the number of individuals of a species per unit area (or volume):</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><strong>Population density = Number of individuals / Area (or volume)</strong></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">High density means many individuals per unit area; low density means few. Density affects resource availability, competition, and disease spread.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Population Size</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Population size is the total number of individuals in a population. It is estimated using:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Direct count:</strong> Count all individuals (practical only for small, visible populations)</li>\n  <li><strong>Quadrat method:</strong> Count organisms in sample squares (quadrats) and extrapolate</li>\n  <li><strong>Mark-recapture (Lincoln-Petersen index):</strong> Capture, mark, release, then recapture; use the ratio to estimate total: N = (M × C) / R, where M = marked first catch, C = second catch, R = marked in second catch</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Population Growth</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Population size changes according to:</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><strong>Population change = (Births + Immigration) − (Deaths + Emigration)</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Exponential (J-shaped) growth:</strong> Occurs when resources are unlimited; population grows at an accelerating rate</li>\n  <li><strong>Logistic (S-shaped) growth:</strong> Growth slows as the population approaches the <strong>carrying capacity (K)</strong> — the maximum population size the environment can sustain</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Population Regulation Factors</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Factor Type</th><th>Examples</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Density-dependent (biotic)</td><td>Competition, predation, disease, parasitism</td><td>Effect intensifies as density increases</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Density-independent (abiotic)</td><td>Drought, floods, extreme temperature, fire</td><td>Effect same regardless of density</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The mark-recapture formula is N = (M × C) / R. The four factors affecting population size are Births, Deaths, Immigration, and Emigration (BDIE). Births and Immigration increase population; Deaths and Emigration decrease it.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Population density = number per unit area. Population size is estimated by direct count, quadrat, or mark-recapture methods. Population growth depends on births + immigration – deaths – emigration. J-shaped growth = unlimited resources; S-shaped (logistic) growth = carrying capacity limits. Density-dependent factors (predation, disease) and density-independent factors (abiotic disasters) regulate populations.\n</div>\n  ",
