    "topicCode": "SS1-BIO-13",
    "module": "Ecology",
    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">functioning ecosystem</span> depends on the continuous flow of energy and cycling of materials. Organisms are classified by how they obtain energy: <span class=\"learn-keyword\">autotrophs</span> (producers) and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">heterotrophs</span> (consumers). Energy flows through food chains and food webs, while materials cycle continuously through biogeochemical cycles.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Autotrophy and Heterotrophy in Ecosystems</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Producers (Autotrophs):</strong> Plants and algae that manufacture food via photosynthesis — form the base of all food chains</li>\n  <li><strong>Primary consumers (Herbivores):</strong> Eat producers — e.g., grasshoppers, rabbits</li>\n  <li><strong>Secondary consumers (Carnivores/Omnivores):</strong> Eat primary consumers — e.g., frogs, foxes</li>\n  <li><strong>Tertiary consumers:</strong> Eat secondary consumers — e.g., eagles</li>\n  <li><strong>Decomposers:</strong> Break down dead organic matter and recycle nutrients — e.g., bacteria, fungi</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Food Chains and Food Webs</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A <strong>food chain</strong> shows the flow of energy from one organism to the next:</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><em>Grass → Grasshopper → Frog → Snake → Eagle</em></p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A <strong>food web</strong> is a network of interconnected food chains, showing the more realistic feeding relationships in an ecosystem. It provides stability — if one species disappears, others can compensate.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Trophic Levels</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Each step in a food chain is a <strong>trophic level</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Trophic Level</th><th>Organism Type</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>1st (T1)</td><td>Producers</td><td>Grass, algae</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>2nd (T2)</td><td>Primary consumers (Herbivores)</td><td>Grasshopper, rabbit</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>3rd (T3)</td><td>Secondary consumers</td><td>Frog, fox</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>4th (T4)</td><td>Tertiary consumers</td><td>Snake, eagle</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Decomposers</td><td>Decomposers/Detritivores</td><td>Bacteria, fungi, earthworms</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Energy Flow and the 10% Rule</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Energy enters ecosystems through photosynthesis and is lost at each trophic level as heat during respiration. Only about <strong>10% of the energy</strong> at one trophic level is transferred to the next. This is why food chains rarely exceed 4–5 levels and why pyramids of energy are always upright.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Energy flows in ONE direction through food chains (producer → consumer). Materials (carbon, nitrogen, water) cycle. Remember the 10% rule: only 10% of energy passes from one level to the next (90% is lost as heat). Arrows in food chains point in the direction of energy flow — from prey to predator.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Ecosystems function through energy flow (one-way) and nutrient cycling. Producers (autotrophs) make food; consumers (heterotrophs) eat others; decomposers recycle nutrients. Food chains show energy pathways; food webs show complexity. Five trophic levels: producers, primary, secondary, tertiary consumers, and decomposers. Only ~10% of energy transfers between trophic levels.\n</div>\n  ",
