    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Energy constantly flows and transforms through ecosystems. The sun is the primary source of energy for almost all life. Through <span class=\"learn-keyword\">photosynthesis</span>, producers convert solar energy into chemical energy. This energy is then transferred through <span class=\"learn-keyword\">food chains</span> and measured as <span class=\"learn-keyword\">productivity</span>. Understanding energy transformation is essential for managing ecosystems.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Energy Flow Through Ecosystems</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Source:</strong> Sunlight (solar energy) is the primary energy source</li>\n  <li>Plants convert ~1–2% of incoming solar energy into chemical energy via photosynthesis</li>\n  <li>Energy is transferred along food chains as organisms eat one another</li>\n  <li>At each transfer, ~90% is lost as heat (respiration, movement, heat loss)</li>\n  <li>Energy flow is <strong>unidirectional</strong> — it does not cycle</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Pyramid of Energy</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">pyramid of energy</span> (kcal/m²/year) shows the total energy at each trophic level per unit area per year. It is <strong>always upright</strong> (widest at producer level, narrowest at top consumer) because energy is always lost at each transfer.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Unlike pyramids of numbers or biomass, the pyramid of energy is NEVER inverted.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Productivity</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Gross Primary Productivity (GPP)</td><td>Total rate of photosynthesis — total chemical energy fixed by producers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Net Primary Productivity (NPP)</td><td>GPP minus energy used by the plant in its own respiration: NPP = GPP − Respiration</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Secondary Productivity</td><td>Rate of energy storage by consumers (animals)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Biogeochemical Cycles</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Unlike energy, materials (carbon, nitrogen, water) <strong>cycle</strong> continuously through biotic and abiotic components:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Carbon cycle:</strong> CO₂ fixation by photosynthesis → respiration/decomposition releases CO₂ back</li>\n  <li><strong>Nitrogen cycle:</strong> N₂ fixation (by bacteria) → nitrification → assimilation → denitrification</li>\n  <li><strong>Water cycle:</strong> Evaporation/transpiration → condensation → precipitation → runoff/infiltration</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Key difference: ENERGY flows one way (sun → producers → consumers → heat). MATERIALS cycle (carbon, nitrogen, water cycle repeatedly). NPP = GPP − Respiration. The pyramid of energy is ALWAYS upright — it can never be inverted because energy is always lost at each trophic level.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Solar energy enters ecosystems via photosynthesis; only ~10% transfers between trophic levels. Energy flow is one-directional. Pyramids of energy are always upright. NPP = GPP − Respiration. Unlike energy, carbon, nitrogen, and water cycle through biotic and abiotic components continuously.\n</div>\n  ",
