    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Micro-organisms</span> (microbes) are living organisms that are too small to be seen with the naked eye and require a microscope to be observed. They are the most numerous, diverse, and widespread forms of life on Earth, found in virtually every habitat including soil, water, air, and living organisms.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Micro-organisms</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Kingdom</th><th>Cell Type</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Bacteria</td><td>Monera</td><td>Prokaryotic</td><td>E. coli, Salmonella, Rhizobium, Lactobacillus</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fungi (microscopic)</td><td>Fungi</td><td>Eukaryotic</td><td>Yeast, Penicillium, Aspergillus</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Protozoa</td><td>Protista</td><td>Eukaryotic</td><td>Amoeba, Paramecium, Plasmodium, Trypanosoma</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Algae (microscopic)</td><td>Protista/Plantae</td><td>Eukaryotic</td><td>Chlamydomonas, Spirogyra, Chlorella</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Viruses</td><td>Borderline</td><td>Non-cellular</td><td>HIV, influenza, polio, hepatitis B</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Characteristics of Micro-organisms</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Microscopic in size (measured in micrometres, µm)</li>\n  <li>Reproduce rapidly (bacteria can double every 20 minutes)</li>\n  <li>Found in diverse habitats — soil, water, air, body surfaces, digestive tracts</li>\n  <li>Can be autotrophic (photosynthetic algae, some bacteria) or heterotrophic</li>\n  <li>Survive harsh conditions through spore formation (bacteria, fungi)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Importance of Micro-organisms</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Decomposition/nutrient cycling</td><td>Bacteria and fungi decompose dead matter, releasing nutrients</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nitrogen fixation</td><td>Rhizobium, Azotobacter fix N₂ → soil fertility</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Food production</td><td>Yeast (bread, beer); Lactobacillus (yogurt, cheese)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Medicine</td><td>Penicillium produces penicillin; bacteria produce insulin (via genetic engineering)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Disease causation</td><td>Bacteria (tuberculosis, cholera); viruses (HIV, malaria parasite is a protozoan)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Biotechnology</td><td>Bacteria used to produce enzymes, hormones, biofuels</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Not all microorganisms are harmful! Many are beneficial: decomposers recycle nutrients; nitrogen-fixing bacteria improve soil fertility; yeast is used in baking and brewing; Lactobacillus makes yogurt; Penicillium produces the antibiotic penicillin. Viruses are the only non-cellular microorganisms.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Micro-organisms include bacteria (prokaryotes), fungi (yeasts, moulds), protozoa, algae, and viruses. They are found everywhere, reproduce rapidly, and are of enormous importance. Beneficial roles include decomposition, nitrogen fixation, food production, and medicine. Harmful roles include causing diseases. Viruses are non-cellular and on the borderline of life.\n</div>\n  ",
