    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Micro-organisms play critical roles in nature and human life through processes such as <span class=\"learn-keyword\">fermentation</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">decay</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">food spoilage</span>, and the causation of <span class=\"learn-keyword\">diseases</span>. Understanding these roles enables us to harness beneficial microbes and protect ourselves from harmful ones.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Fermentation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Fermentation is the anaerobic breakdown of organic compounds (mainly carbohydrates) by micro-organisms to produce useful products:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Micro-organism</th><th>Substrate</th><th>Products</th><th>Application</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Yeast (Saccharomyces)</td><td>Sugars</td><td>Ethanol + CO₂</td><td>Bread, beer, wine</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Lactobacillus</td><td>Lactose (milk sugar)</td><td>Lactic acid</td><td>Yogurt, cheese, silage</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Acetobacter</td><td>Ethanol</td><td>Acetic acid</td><td>Vinegar production</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Methanogenic bacteria</td><td>Organic waste</td><td>Methane (CH₄)</td><td>Biogas fuel</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Decay and Decomposition</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Bacteria and fungi (decomposers) break down dead organic matter in a process called <strong>decay</strong></li>\n  <li>They secrete enzymes that digest organic compounds externally, then absorb the products</li>\n  <li>Decay releases CO₂, water, ammonia, and mineral salts — essential for nutrient cycling</li>\n  <li>Conditions favouring decay: warmth, moisture, oxygen (for aerobic decomposers), neutral pH</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Food Spoilage</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Food spoilage occurs when micro-organisms grow in food, making it unfit for consumption:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Bacteria:</strong> Produce toxins (e.g., Clostridium botulinum produces botulin toxin in canned food)</li>\n  <li><strong>Moulds (fungi):</strong> Produce mycotoxins (e.g., Aspergillus flavus produces aflatoxin on groundnuts)</li>\n  <li>Food preservation methods prevent spoilage: refrigeration, drying, salting, canning, pasteurisation, irradiation, pickling</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Micro-organisms as Disease Agents</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Pathogen</th><th>Type</th><th>Disease</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</td><td>Bacterium</td><td>Tuberculosis (TB)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Vibrio cholerae</td><td>Bacterium</td><td>Cholera</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Salmonella typhi</td><td>Bacterium</td><td>Typhoid fever</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Plasmodium falciparum</td><td>Protozoan</td><td>Malaria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>HIV</td><td>Virus</td><td>AIDS</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Poliovirus</td><td>Virus</td><td>Poliomyelitis</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Know key examples: Yeast → ethanol + CO₂ (bread/brewing); Lactobacillus → lactic acid (yogurt); Aflatoxin from Aspergillus flavus on groundnuts is a carcinogen. Conditions that slow decay/spoilage: low temperature, low moisture, low pH, absence of oxygen, high salt/sugar concentration.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Microbes act through fermentation (yeast: ethanol; Lactobacillus: lactic acid), decay (decomposers break down dead matter, cycling nutrients), food spoilage (bacteria and moulds make food unsafe), and disease (bacteria, protozoa, viruses cause infections). Food preservation methods inhibit microbial growth.\n</div>\n  ",
