    "topic": "Towards Better Health",
    "topicCode": "SS1-BIO-18",
    "module": "Health Biology",
    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Good health is defined by the World Health Organization (WHO) as 'a state of complete physical, mental, and social well-being, not merely the absence of disease.' Achieving and maintaining good health requires proper <span class=\"learn-keyword\">hygiene</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">sanitation</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">vaccination</span>, and effective <span class=\"learn-keyword\">disease prevention</span> strategies.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Hygiene</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Hygiene is the practice of maintaining cleanliness to prevent disease. Types include:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Personal hygiene:</strong> Regular bathing, hand washing, clean clothes, dental care</li>\n  <li><strong>Food hygiene:</strong> Proper storage, cooking, and handling of food to prevent contamination</li>\n  <li><strong>Environmental hygiene:</strong> Keeping surroundings clean to reduce breeding sites for pathogens and vectors</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Sanitation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Sanitation involves the provision and maintenance of facilities for safe disposal of human waste and contamination control:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Safe water supply and water treatment (chlorination, boiling)</li>\n  <li>Proper sewage disposal (pit latrines, sewage treatment plants)</li>\n  <li>Refuse disposal (waste management, landfills)</li>\n  <li>Control of disease vectors (mosquitoes, rats, flies) through drainage, insecticides, and nets</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Vaccination (Immunisation)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Vaccination stimulates the body's immune system to produce <strong>antibodies</strong> against specific pathogens without causing the disease. Types:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Active immunity:</strong> Vaccination or natural infection stimulates the body to make its own antibodies — long-lasting</li>\n  <li><strong>Passive immunity:</strong> Ready-made antibodies given directly (e.g., anti-tetanus serum) — immediate but short-lived</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Vaccine</th><th>Disease prevented</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>BCG</td><td>Tuberculosis</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>OPV (Oral Polio Vaccine)</td><td>Poliomyelitis</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>DPT</td><td>Diphtheria, Pertussis (whooping cough), Tetanus</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>MMR</td><td>Measles, Mumps, Rubella</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Hepatitis B vaccine</td><td>Hepatitis B</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Disease Prevention Strategies</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Primary prevention:</strong> Stop disease before it occurs (vaccination, hygiene, vector control)</li>\n  <li><strong>Secondary prevention:</strong> Early detection and treatment (screening, diagnosis)</li>\n  <li><strong>Tertiary prevention:</strong> Reduce impact of established disease (rehabilitation, management)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Key distinction: Active immunity = body makes own antibodies (long-lasting). Passive immunity = antibodies given from outside (immediate but short-lived). Vaccination is active immunity. Anti-tetanus serum (given after a wound) is passive immunity.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Better health is achieved through: personal, food, and environmental hygiene; sanitation (safe water, sewage disposal, waste management, vector control); vaccination (active immunity — long-lasting; passive immunity — immediate, short-lived); and disease prevention at primary, secondary, and tertiary levels.\n</div>\n  ",
