    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">habitat</span> is the natural environment in which an organism lives and obtains all its requirements — food, water, shelter, and space for reproduction. Different habitats are characterised by distinct physical conditions that determine which organisms can survive there.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Habitat</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A habitat provides an organism with:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Suitable abiotic conditions (temperature, light, water, soil type)</li>\n  <li>Food sources (prey, plants, or nutrients)</li>\n  <li>Shelter and sites for reproduction</li>\n  <li>Other organisms of the same species for reproduction</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Habitats</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Major Type</th><th>Subtypes</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Aquatic</td><td>Freshwater, Marine, Estuarine, Brackish</td><td>River, lake, ocean, estuary</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Terrestrial</td><td>Forest, Grassland, Desert, Tundra</td><td>Tropical rainforest, savanna, Sahara, Arctic</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Arboreal</td><td>Tree-dwelling</td><td>Monkeys, chameleons, tree snakes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Subterranean</td><td>Underground/Soil</td><td>Earthworms, moles, soil bacteria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Aerial</td><td>Air/sky</td><td>Birds, bats, insects in flight</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Characteristics of Habitats</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Each habitat has unique abiotic characteristics that determine which organisms can live there:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Aquatic habitats:</strong> Water availability, dissolved oxygen, salinity, water current, depth, temperature, pH</li>\n  <li><strong>Terrestrial habitats:</strong> Temperature, rainfall, soil type, humidity, wind, topography</li>\n  <li><strong>Adaptation:</strong> Organisms have structural, physiological, and behavioural adaptations to their habitat</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Habitat and Ecological Niche</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Habitat = WHERE an organism lives. Niche = HOW an organism lives (its role, diet, behaviour, interactions). Two species can share the same habitat but occupy different niches. No two species can occupy identical niches indefinitely (competitive exclusion principle).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: Habitat = WHERE an organism lives (the address). Niche = HOW it lives (the occupation/role). A pond is a habitat; a frog in a pond eating insects is occupying its niche. Major habitat divisions: aquatic (water) and terrestrial (land).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> A habitat is where an organism lives and finds its requirements. Major types: aquatic (freshwater, marine, estuarine), terrestrial (forest, grassland, desert, tundra), arboreal (trees), subterranean (soil/underground), and aerial. Organisms are adapted to their habitats. Habitat = where (address); niche = role (occupation).\n</div>\n  ",
