    "topic": "Aquatic Habitat",
    "topicCode": "SS1-BIO-20",
    "module": "Ecology",
    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Aquatic habitats</span> are environments where water is the primary medium for life. They cover about 71% of Earth's surface and support enormous biodiversity. The two main categories are <span class=\"learn-keyword\">freshwater</span> (rivers, lakes, ponds) and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">marine</span> (oceans, seas), with <span class=\"learn-keyword\">estuarine</span> environments at the interface.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Freshwater Habitat</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Low salinity (less than 0.5 ppt)</li>\n  <li>Includes lotic (flowing: rivers, streams) and lentic (still: lakes, ponds) environments</li>\n  <li><strong>Abiotic factors:</strong> Temperature, dissolved oxygen, pH, turbidity, current speed, light penetration</li>\n  <li><strong>Organisms:</strong> Tilapia, catfish, frogs, water hyacinth, algae, insect larvae, Amoeba</li>\n  <li>Organisms face osmotic challenges — freshwater organisms tend to gain water by osmosis</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Estuarine Habitat</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">An estuary is where a river meets the sea — characterized by <strong>fluctuating salinity</strong> (brackish water), tidal flows, nutrient-rich waters, and variable temperature.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Very productive ecosystem due to high nutrient input from both land and sea</li>\n  <li>Important nursery ground for many fish species</li>\n  <li>Organisms must be euryhaline (tolerant of wide salinity range): mudskippers, oysters, mangroves, crabs</li>\n  <li>Mangrove forests stabilize estuarine banks and provide habitat</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Marine Habitat</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>High salinity (~3.5% or 35 ppt)</li>\n  <li>Zones: intertidal (between tide marks), neritic (shallow coastal), pelagic (open water), benthic (sea floor), abyssal (deep sea)</li>\n  <li><strong>Organisms:</strong> Plankton, fish, whales, sharks, coral, sea anemones, jellyfish, starfish</li>\n  <li>Marine organisms have adaptations to resist dehydration caused by osmosis in high-salinity water</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Adaptations to Aquatic Life</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Organism</th><th>Adaptation</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Fish</td><td>Streamlined body, gills, fins, lateral line</td><td>Movement, breathing, sensing</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Aquatic plants</td><td>Submerged leaves finely dissected; air spaces (aerenchyma)</td><td>Reduce resistance; buoyancy; gas exchange</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Freshwater protozoa</td><td>Contractile vacuole</td><td>Expel excess water gained by osmosis</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Marine fish</td><td>Drink large amounts of water; produce little concentrated urine</td><td>Counteract osmotic water loss</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Estuary = where river meets sea = fluctuating salinity. Freshwater organisms gain water by osmosis (need to expel it — e.g., contractile vacuole in Amoeba). Marine organisms lose water by osmosis (need to drink water). Estuarine organisms must tolerate both extremes (euryhaline).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Aquatic habitats include freshwater (low salinity, rivers/ponds), estuarine (fluctuating salinity, where river meets sea), and marine (high salinity, oceans). Key adaptations: streamlined bodies, gills, fins (fish); contractile vacuoles (freshwater protozoa); aerenchyma (aquatic plants). Estuaries are highly productive and important as fish nurseries.\n</div>\n  ",
