    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">terrestrial habitat</span> is a land-based environment where organisms live. Terrestrial habitats are the most diverse on Earth — ranging from tropical rainforests and savannas to deserts and tundra — each shaped by climate, soil, and topography.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Terrestrial Habitats</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Habitat Type</th><th>Climate</th><th>Key Plants</th><th>Key Animals</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Tropical Rainforest</td><td>Hot, very high rainfall (>200 cm/yr), no dry season</td><td>Tall canopy trees, epiphytes, ferns</td><td>Monkeys, parrots, leopards, frogs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Savanna (Guinea savanna)</td><td>Warm, seasonal rainfall, long dry season</td><td>Grasses, scattered trees (acacia, baobab)</td><td>Elephants, lions, zebra, grass-cutters</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Desert</td><td>Extremely low rainfall (<25 cm/yr), extreme temperatures</td><td>Cacti, succulents, thorn scrub</td><td>Camels, scorpions, desert foxes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Temperate Forest</td><td>Moderate rainfall, four seasons</td><td>Deciduous trees (oak, beech, maple)</td><td>Deer, badgers, foxes, owls</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tundra</td><td>Very cold, low rainfall, permafrost</td><td>Mosses, lichens, low shrubs</td><td>Polar bears, arctic foxes, caribou</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Soil — The Foundation of Terrestrial Habitats</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Soil provides anchorage, water, and mineral nutrients for plants</li>\n  <li>Soil components: mineral particles (sand, silt, clay), humus (organic matter), water, air, and organisms</li>\n  <li>Loam (mixture of sand, silt, clay) is ideal for plant growth</li>\n  <li><strong>Humus:</strong> Decomposed organic matter — improves soil fertility, water-holding capacity, and aeration</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Adaptations to Terrestrial Life</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Organism</th><th>Challenge</th><th>Adaptation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Desert plants (cacti)</td><td>Water scarcity</td><td>Thick stem stores water; spines reduce transpiration; deep roots</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Deciduous trees</td><td>Cold winter (water frozen)</td><td>Shed leaves in autumn to reduce water loss and damage</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Camels</td><td>Desert heat and water scarcity</td><td>Hump stores fat; efficient kidneys; wide feet for sand</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Burrowing animals</td><td>Extreme surface temperatures</td><td>Burrow underground for insulation</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Biotic and Abiotic Interactions</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Terrestrial food chains typically start with grasses or trees (producers)</li>\n  <li>Decomposers (earthworms, bacteria, fungi) in soil are critical for nutrient recycling</li>\n  <li>Key abiotic factors: temperature, rainfall, soil type, humidity, wind, light</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Key challenge for terrestrial organisms = WATER CONSERVATION (unlike aquatic organisms). Desert adaptations all relate to reducing water loss and surviving heat. Loam soil is the best agricultural soil because it has a balanced mix of particles and good water-holding capacity.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Terrestrial habitats range from tropical rainforests to deserts and tundra. Soil provides nutrients, anchorage, and water. Key challenge for land organisms is water conservation. Adaptations: cacti store water; deciduous trees shed leaves; camels have efficient kidneys. Soil components: mineral particles + humus + water + air + organisms.\n</div>\n  ",
