    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Grassland habitats</span> are terrestrial ecosystems dominated by grasses and herbs, with few or no trees. They cover about 25% of Earth's land surface and are found on every continent except Antarctica. In Nigeria and West Africa, the Guinea Savanna is the main grassland type.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Grasslands</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Location/Climate</th><th>Key Features</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Savanna</td><td>Tropical, seasonal rainfall, Africa/S.America/Asia</td><td>Grass dominant; scattered trees; seasonal fires</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Prairie</td><td>Temperate, North America</td><td>Tall grasses; deep, fertile soil; no trees</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Steppe</td><td>Temperate, Eurasia</td><td>Short, dry grasses; cold winters</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pampas</td><td>Temperate, South America</td><td>Tall grasses; fertile soil</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Characteristics of Grassland Habitats</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Dominated by grasses (Poaceae family) and herbaceous plants</li>\n  <li>Rainfall: 25–75 cm/year (more than desert, less than forest)</li>\n  <li>Seasonal drought and periodic fires maintain the grassland (prevent tree growth)</li>\n  <li>Rich, deep soils with abundant humus from decomposing grass litter</li>\n  <li>High biodiversity, especially of large herbivores, birds, and insects</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Adaptations of Grassland Organisms</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Organism</th><th>Adaptation</th><th>Benefit</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Grasses</td><td>Meristem at base of leaves; deep fibrous roots</td><td>Regrow after grazing or fire; resist drought</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Large herbivores (zebra, cattle)</td><td>Flat, grinding molar teeth; long digestive tract with cellulose-digesting bacteria</td><td>Efficiently process tough grass fibers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ground-nesting birds</td><td>Camouflaged feathers</td><td>Protection from predators on open ground</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Burrowing animals (aardvark, prairie dog)</td><td>Strong limbs for digging</td><td>Shelter from predators and temperature extremes</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Ecological Importance of Grasslands</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Carbon storage — grassland soils store large amounts of carbon</li>\n  <li>Watershed protection — prevent soil erosion and regulate water flow</li>\n  <li>Biodiversity hotspots — support diverse food webs</li>\n  <li>Agriculture — converted to croplands and pastures (wheat, maize, sorghum cultivation)</li>\n  <li>Grassland degradation (overgrazing, farming) leads to soil erosion and desertification</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Key grassland features: grass meristem at BASE of leaf (not tip) — this is why grass can be grazed or mown and still grow back. Fires and grazing MAINTAIN grasslands by preventing tree colonisation. Grassland soils are among the most fertile on Earth due to deep humus accumulation.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Grasslands (savanna, prairie, steppe) are dominated by grasses with 25–75 cm/year rainfall. Seasonal drought and fire maintain them. Grass meristems at leaf bases allow regrowth after grazing/fire. Herbivores have grinding teeth and cellulose-digesting gut bacteria. Grasslands have fertile soils, store carbon, and are important for agriculture. Overgrazing threatens them with desertification.\n</div>\n  ",
