    "topic": "Reproduction in Unicellular Organisms and Invertebrates I",
    "topicCode": "SS1-BIO-23",
    "module": "Reproduction",
    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Reproduction</span> is the biological process by which organisms produce new individuals of the same species, ensuring the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">continuity of life</span>. There are two fundamental types: <span class=\"learn-keyword\">asexual reproduction</span> (involving only one parent, no fusion of gametes) and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">sexual reproduction</span> (involving fusion of male and female gametes). In this topic, we focus on reproduction in unicellular organisms and simpler invertebrates.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Reproduction</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Asexual Reproduction</th><th>Sexual Reproduction</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Parents involved</td><td>One parent</td><td>Usually two parents</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Gametes</td><td>No gametes</td><td>Fusion of gametes (fertilization)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Genetic variation</td><td>Offspring genetically identical (clones)</td><td>Offspring genetically varied</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Speed</td><td>Rapid</td><td>Usually slower</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Examples</td><td>Binary fission, budding, spore formation</td><td>Conjugation, fertilization</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Reproduction in Unicellular Organisms</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Amoeba (Binary Fission):</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Nucleus divides (mitosis) → cytoplasm divides → two identical daughter cells</li>\n  <li>Asexual; very rapid; daughter cells are clones of parent</li>\n  <li>Also forms cysts (dormant spores) under unfavorable conditions</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Paramecium:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Asexual:</strong> Binary fission (transverse) — divides across the middle</li>\n  <li><strong>Sexual:</strong> Conjugation — two Paramecia pair up, exchange micronuclei (genetic material), then separate and divide</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Yeast (Budding):</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>A small bud grows from the parent cell</li>\n  <li>Nucleus divides; one nucleus enters bud</li>\n  <li>Bud grows and eventually separates to form daughter cell</li>\n  <li>Asexual; rapid population growth under favorable conditions</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Reproduction in Simple Invertebrates</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Hydra (Budding):</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>A small bud grows from the parent's body wall by repeated mitosis</li>\n  <li>Develops tentacles and mouth, then detaches as a new individual</li>\n  <li>Also reproduces sexually (separate male and female gonads) during unfavorable conditions</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><strong>Spirogyra (Conjugation):</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>A filamentous green alga that reproduces sexually by conjugation</li>\n  <li>Two filaments align; conjugation tubes form; cell contents (gametes) from one filament move into the other</li>\n  <li>Zygospores form, which are resistant and can survive unfavorable conditions</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Binary fission = asexual reproduction in bacteria and Amoeba (one parent → two identical daughters). Budding = asexual reproduction in yeast and Hydra (bud grows off parent). Conjugation = sexual reproduction in Paramecium and Spirogyra (genetic material exchanged). Sexual reproduction produces genetic variation; asexual produces clones.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Asexual reproduction: binary fission (Amoeba, bacteria), budding (yeast, Hydra). Sexual reproduction: conjugation (Paramecium, Spirogyra). Asexual = one parent, clones, fast. Sexual = two parents, genetic variation, usually slower. Most organisms switch to sexual reproduction under unfavorable conditions to generate variation for adaptability.\n</div>\n  ",
