    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  In this second part on reproduction, we examine <span class=\"learn-keyword\">spore formation</span> in fungi and ferns, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">fragmentation</span> and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">regeneration</span>, and reproduction in more complex invertebrates such as earthworms (hermaphroditic) and insects (metamorphosis). We also compare <span class=\"learn-keyword\">complete</span> and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">incomplete metamorphosis</span>.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Spore Formation (Sporulation)</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Spores are small, light, resistant reproductive structures produced in large numbers</li>\n  <li><strong>Fungi (e.g., Rhizopus, Mucor):</strong> Produce asexual spores in sporangia — released and dispersed by wind; germinate in suitable conditions</li>\n  <li><strong>Ferns:</strong> Produce spores on the underside of fronds (in sori); spores germinate into gametophytes</li>\n  <li><strong>Bacteria:</strong> Form endospores under adverse conditions — not for reproduction, but survival</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Fragmentation and Regeneration</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Fragmentation:</strong> An organism breaks into pieces, each developing into a complete new individual</li>\n  <li>Examples: Spirogyra (filament breaks into fragments, each grows into new filament); Planaria (flatworm); starfish</li>\n  <li><strong>Regeneration:</strong> Regrowth of lost body parts — a survival ability, not primarily reproductive</li>\n  <li>Examples: earthworm (limited regeneration), lizard tail regrowth, starfish from a severed arm</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Earthworm Reproduction</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Earthworms are <strong>hermaphrodites</strong> — each individual has both male and female reproductive organs</li>\n  <li>They still cross-fertilize (two individuals mate, exchanging sperm)</li>\n  <li>The clitellum secretes a mucus cocoon in which fertilized eggs develop</li>\n  <li>Young worms emerge fully formed (direct development — no larval stage)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Insect Metamorphosis</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Metamorphosis is the transformation of an insect through distinct developmental stages:</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Complete Metamorphosis (Holometabolism)</th><th>Incomplete Metamorphosis (Hemimetabolism)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Stages</td><td>Egg → Larva → Pupa → Adult (4 stages)</td><td>Egg → Nymph → Adult (3 stages)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Larva/Nymph</td><td>Larva looks completely different from adult (e.g., caterpillar vs butterfly)</td><td>Nymph resembles small adult (no wings yet)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pupa</td><td>Present (major transformation occurs)</td><td>Absent</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Examples</td><td>Butterfly, moth, housefly, mosquito, beetle</td><td>Grasshopper, cockroach, locust, dragonfly</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Complete metamorphosis = 4 stages (Egg → Larva → Pupa → Adult). Incomplete = 3 stages (Egg → Nymph → Adult). In complete metamorphosis, larva and adult are morphologically very different and often occupy different ecological niches. Key examples: butterfly (complete); grasshopper (incomplete).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Spore formation: fungi (in sporangia) and ferns. Fragmentation: Spirogyra, Planaria, starfish (each piece grows into new individual). Earthworms: hermaphrodites, cross-fertilize, clitellum forms cocoon. Complete metamorphosis: 4 stages (egg → larva → pupa → adult) e.g., butterfly. Incomplete metamorphosis: 3 stages (egg → nymph → adult) e.g., grasshopper.\n</div>\n  ",
